{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And the days of Israel drew near to die.</b> In the Midrash (Tanchuma, Vayechi 7) they said: the days die but they do not die — for as long as the righteous person is in the world, the days of tranquility are found in the world, and when he dies those days of tranquility are nullified, and their nullification is their death. And as they said: as long as Jacob was alive the enslavement did not begin. But Jacob himself — our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said that he did not die. And even though every righteous person is called alive in his death, nevertheless Jacob specifically was chosen, because they expounded about him (Taanit 5b): he is likened to his seed — just as his seed is alive, so too he is alive. For the son has within him a portion of his father, and every offspring is the thigh of its mother and its father. And when his seed is alive, then it is as though he too is alive — I mean, a portion of him. And specifically Jacob, whose bed was complete and all of them were living seed, active and manifold. But one who begets a wicked son, who in his lifetime is called dead — then there is no offspring to merit the father, for the inference from the premise suffices to match the conclusion, and he himself is called dead in his lifetime, so how can the portion of his father be called alive? And this is what they expounded (Bava Batra 116a) on the verse: ״וְהֲדַד שָׁמַע בְּמִצְרַיִם כִּי שָׁכַב דָּוִד עִם אֲבוֹתָיו וְכִי מֵת יוֹאָב״ (I Kings 11:21) — why did it mention lying down for David and death for Joab? Because David left behind a righteous son like himself, which was not so with Joab. And for this reason lying down was said of Jacob, and death was said only of the days." "<b>And you shall do with me kindness and truth, etc.</b> There is here a request on the side of truth and on the side of kindness. On the side of truth — that is, a matter that does not expect repayment of reward; and on the side of kindness — that is, a matter that does expect repayment of reward. For the request he made of him to bury him — this is a matter that expects repayment of reward, as is derived (Mo'ed Katan 28b): 'they shall surely bury him,' etc. But what he asked of him — to bury him in the land of Canaan — this is a kindness that does not expect repayment of reward. Another matter: what he asked, that they not bury him in Egypt so they would not make him an object of foreign worship — this is on the side of truth; he was obligated to do so to remove a stumbling block from the Egyptians. But what he asked — that they bury him specifically in the land of Canaan — this is on the side of kindness." "And as for what Rashi explained — three reasons for the matter — it appears that one may explain that all of them depend on one another. For the reason that they not make him an object of foreign worship, and that the lice not gain dominion over his grave — it is all one reason: for why would they make him an object of foreign worship, and what is one man more than another? Rather, because they would see that all the dust of the earth is lice except for his grave, therefore they would stray after him; and if the lice were also on his grave, he would suffer distress from them. And the reason of lice alone does not suffice, for one could object: but is there not much relief and deliverance before the Holy One, blessed be He, to save him from the lice — for our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Bava Batra 17a) that over seven the worm did not gain dominion, and Jacob is one of them, and if the worm did not gain dominion over him, then certainly not the lice. Rather, he said that if the lice were not on his grave they would make him an object of foreign worship. And with these two reasons alone it does not suffice, for if so, why did he command that they bury him specifically in the land of Canaan and not in other lands? Rather, it was because of the distress of the rolling of the burial-places. And with the reason of the rolling of burial-places alone it does not suffice, for if so, why did he say ״אַל תִּקְבְּרֵנִי בְּמִצְרָיִם״ (Genesis 47:29) — it would be enough for me with what he said, ״וּקְבַרְתַּנִי בִּקְבֻרָתָם״ (Genesis 47:30). Rather, he said: I request of you that you bury me in the graves of my fathers, and if some cause or impediment comes to your hand so that you are unable to bury me in the land of Canaan, then bury me in whatever land it may be except for the land of Egypt — do not bury me there at all. And why all this? Rather, it is certainly because of the concern of foreign worship or lice, as stated above."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:34:13.895069633Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}